[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Database Integration Testing with JUnit 5, Testcontainers, and Spring Boot"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Integration testing for data layers requires a reliable, isolated database instance. Relying on an external shared database or an in-memory database like H2 often leads to false positives due to dialect differences. This tutorial explores how to combine JUnit 5, Testcontainers, @Sql script execution, and transaction management to build robust, production-grade database integration tests."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Project Dependencies"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To get started, ensure your build file includes Spring Boot Starter Test, Testcontainers for your specific database (PostgreSQL in this example), and the JUnit 5 platform dependencies."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"testImplementation("org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-test")
testImplementation("org.testcontainers:junit-jupiter")
testImplementation("org.testcontainers:postgresql")
testImplementation("org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-testcontainers")"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Configuring Testcontainers with Spring Boot"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Boot 3.1+ provides native support for Testcontainers using the @ServiceConnection annotation. This automatically configures the DataSource properties, removing the need for manual PropertySource initializers."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class UserRepositoryIntegrationTest {

    @Container
    @ServiceConnection
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15-alpine");

    // Test methods go here
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Seeding Data with @Sql"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @Sql annotation allows you to populate the database with known datasets before running your tests. You can target specific methods or place it at the class level."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@Test
@Sql(scripts = "/sql/insert-users.sql")
void shouldFindUserByEmail() {
    Optional<User> user = userRepository.findByEmail("test@example.com");
    assertThat(user).isPresent();
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Managing Transactions and Isolation"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, if you annotate your test class or method with @Transactional, Spring will wrap each test in a transaction and rollback the changes afterward. This ensures test isolation. However, be cautious when using Testcontainers with lazy database initializations or when testing asynchronous code."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
@Transactional
class ProductRepositoryTest {

    @Autowired
    private ProductRepository productRepository;

    @Test
    @Sql(statements = "INSERT INTO products (id, name, price) VALUES (1, 'Laptop', 1200.00);")
    void shouldPersistAndRollbackTransaction() {
        var product = productRepository.findById(1L);
        assertThat(product).isPresent();
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Database Testing"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use @ServiceConnection to reduce boilerplate configuration for Testcontainers."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Combine @Sql execution with @Transactional rollback to keep tests fast and completely independent."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use container reuse features (testcontainers.reuse.enable=true) locally to speed up test execution cycles."}]
